  {
    "episode_id": 84135,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84135.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "Larry, it's a very important question, a great question. I believe we need to democratize the access to the technology economy. Right now, it's basically four or five super cities, LA, San Jose, Boston, Austin. There's no reason, especially post COVID, where so many people are working remotely and where companies have realized you can do remote work, that we can't expand these opportunities to have these innovation jobs in rural communities and minority communities. Enrico Moretti, the economist, has shown that they have a multiplier effect. So if you have a core group of innovation jobs that leads to jobs in welding, jobs in construction, jobs in manufacturing and services. So our challenge first is how do we give more people these opportunities, more people basic credentialing, and creating more tech hubs across the country. The second thing is we have to protect people's privacy and dignity. I mean, people need to understand when they're online that their data won't be manipulated, that their information won't be stored forever. And those are things that I've come up with an internet bill of rights that Tim Berners-Lee was one of the founders of the web that I hope we will consider. And then the final point where we need better understanding is to make sure social media is a force for improving deliberation, not hurting democracy. I mean, yes, we've given everyone access to speech, but the speech shouldn't just be applying propaganda or misinformation or outrage. And how do we create healthy forums in the 21st century that actually are encouraging conversation. Audrey Tang has done this in Taiwan. It's an impressive model. We have to consider how do we do the liberty forms here in the United States.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_11",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
