    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84135,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84135.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's a great question. How do we do that? I mean, community banks are 97% of banks in this country, as you know. But they only have 16% of the assets. Total assets are 18 trillion. And you look at the Small Business Administration in a typical year. They give $30 billion of loans. That's a drop in the bucket when you look at 18 trillion. So what I think we ought to do is have the federal reserve loan to community banks directly. They can keep the small business loan as collateral, but they're allowed to loan And if they loan to small businesses and keep the small business loan as collateral, they will free up a lot of these community banks to be able to give loans to small and medium-sized companies, many of whom don't want to be Google or Facebook, but want to have a 2x, 3x return and are supporting the livelihood. So that's one way, I think, through Fed policy that we can democratize access to capital. The feds have no problem bailing out corporations or infusing cash towards banks. They should be willing to do that for the community banks.",
