  {
    "episode_id": 84135,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84135.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "Sure, it's basically protecting people online for their privacy and their rights. So before a company can collect information about your data, they would have to have your affirmative consent. You would have to consent. You'd have to have the right to know what's happening to your data. So tomorrow, if you wanted to ask Google or Facebook. What's happened to the data? Who have you transferred it to? You would have a right to know that. You'd have to have your data deleted on those platforms if you wanted that data removed. You'd have the right to transfer your data to somewhere else. You'd have the right to access to the internet. I believe that it's a First Amendment and 14th Amendment right. You can't have actual speech. in America today if you don't have access to the internet and you don't have access to jobs or health care under the 14th Amendment or education unless you have access to the internet. It talks about net neutrality so the internet shouldn't be able to service providers shouldn't be able to block certain content, the need for competition online and the need for non-discrimination. So there are 10 principles but that's the thrust of it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_11",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
