  {
    "episode_id": 84135,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84135.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 76,
    "turn_text": "I appreciate so much your thoughts on this. I was actually at the hearing where Cosy Cortez testified and I'm on that committee with Gerald Connolly and it's very powerful and Reverend Barber also has testified to our committee. I mean, 40% of Americans are low income, low wealth. That means they're less than double the poverty line and they can't afford family supporting wage. They don't have a wage that supports them and their families to have basic health care education. So there are several things we need. We need a massive increase in the minimum wage. We need a massive increase in the earned income tax credit. We need to create more high paying jobs in communities because that has a multiplier effect on supporting other good paying jobs. We need to deal with segregation. For example, in Chicago, there are a lot of tech jobs that have a multiplier effect on service jobs for neighborhoods where those tech jobs are, but they're not doing anything for black neighborhoods that are deeply segregated. They have no multiplier effects. Other segregation policy that has to do with the disparities. And then as you put it for the disabled community, we need In my judgment, the SSI and the SSDI, Social Security Disability Insurance is paltry. I mean, you can't live on $700 a month. The requirements that after $2,000 of savings, you can't say more, whether if you go work part-time, you lose those benefits are absurd. So I have a bill which we're working on to increase dramatically the Social Security disability benefits for those who can't work so that they can have a wage that they can live on.",
