  {
    "episode_id": 84135,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84135.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 84,
    "turn_text": "the very top of points uh... you know james hankman is a noble laureate economists said that very hard to retrain people once they're in their forties or fifties on a new skills but it's a high rate of return if you train people when they're younger and so we can create a lot of credentialing for a tech support for tech design for robotics process automation. And there have been successful pilot programs, People Shores is doing it in Quiksville, Mississippi, Accenture, in Jefferson, Iowa, Beckley West, Virginia is doing it. There are almost 200 and 11,000 of these tech jobs that I think could be done in the United States that were off short. And so there are a lot of skills, and that doesn't mean they have to work at Apple or Google. They could work for a Fortune 500 company. There are a lot of skills, I think, that may not even require a two-year or four-year degree, but would require basic credentialing. But I agree that it's not like we're going to make coal miners' programmers. I think that's an absurd thought. But their kids could go into some of these jobs. And then Enrico Moretti, the economist, has shown that a tech job, innovation job, has a four-times multiplier. So for every one job, you have masseuses and restaurants and construction workers. And so what the idea is that we need to create more high-paying innovation jobs in communities to have a beneficial effect more on the community. But of course it takes more, it's going to take infrastructure investment, it's going to take investment in many other types of areas to create jobs, you can't just rely on innovation. And then I reject the idea of automation, closing jobs in Paul Krugman, the Nobel Laureate has a great essay on it, which is that our productivity rates haven't gone up enough to show that. I mean, if really we were having automation taking out the work, you would have much higher productivity rates in the United States, and so far we haven't seen that. And so what I do think it is is it's putting a disproportionate burden on folks who are traditionally in manufacturing jobs or folks who had jobs that could be automated. But the point is not that it's going to get rid of all jobs, it's that it's going to create different types of jobs. And we have to deal with the inequities of that disruption.",
