  {
    "episode_id": 84135,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84135.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 91,
    "turn_text": "That's a great question. And of course, Martha Essen, the Nobel Laureate economist, and Martha Nussbaum, a famous philosopher, have this approach where they call it the capabilities approach and human development, that they in the 1990s challenged the idea that the economy should be based simply based on GDP. And they came up with all different types of capabilities from our ability to have family, to have ability to have health, or ability to have recreation and play, that are the true measures of a society and a person's well-being. There's a great book by Gene Spirling who was a former economic advisor in Obama and Clinton called Dignity, where he tries to articulate what that approach would look like in the United States. How we shouldn't just be obsessing over the GDP, but really should be figuring out what gives people of potential to have meaningful lives, to have pursue happiness. So I think we have to have that kind of conversation. The philosophers like Amard Desen and Nussbaum have done a lot of the heavy lifting. And now it's for policymakers to look at their ideas, which the UNDP has already done and implement some of those alternative metrics in our economic system.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_11",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
