    "turn_idx": 113,
    "turn_text": "That's a great question. I think Mitt Romney, possibly. I mean, he had plotted the idea of $1,000 payments. That's one person off the top of my head, I could see. Justin Amash tends to be fiscally very conservative. I mean, he's obviously really very good on civil liberties issues and certain foreign policy issues, but I don't think he'd be a logical candidate for this. And then I would just say that the goal, I think, You're absolutely right to point out the flaws, but if you look, talk to someone like Reverend Barber, who's been leading the Poor People's Campaign. He'll tell you the part of the fraud flaws of that. We haven't had dramatic increases in the minimum wage. We haven't had dramatic increases in the ITC. We have these flaws, which you said that SSI and SSDI, you have to be on the wit list. You're not qualifying. 13 million people don't have poverty. So it's my view is that you can also have these programs be much more robust and had Bernie Sanders, who I was a co-chair for, gotten elected. I'm no doubt he would have done that. But there are other times, I think, where in a case of the crisis like this, the most efficient way of getting money out to people who need it. And that's what Tim Ryan I build us.",
